# Deploy {{.Environment}}: what's left
{{- if .IsSmall}}

## 1. Provision the box

Fill in `deployments/{{.Environment}}/terraform/terraform.tfvars`. It's gitignored, like `.env`. The runner token is valid for one hour, so get it right before applying:

```sh
gh api -X POST repos/OWNER/REPO/actions/runners/registration-token --jq .token
cd deployments/{{.Environment}}/terraform
terraform init
terraform apply
```

Cloud-init installs Docker and registers a runner labelled `{{.Environment}}`. Check it shows up under the repo's Settings → Actions → Runners.

## 2. Set up the box

SSH in as `ubuntu` (`terraform output public_ip`), then as root:

```sh
git clone git@github.com:OWNER/REPO.git /root/{{.Name}}
```

and write `/root/{{.Name}}/deployments/{{.Environment}}/.env`:

```sh
ENVIRONMENT={{.Environment}}
ADDRESS=:4000
{{- if .HasPostgres}}
POSTGRES_USER=app
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=CHANGE_ME
POSTGRES_DB={{.Name}}
GOOSE_DBSTRING=postgres://app:CHANGE_ME@postgres:5432/{{.Name}}?sslmode=disable
{{- end}}
{{- if .HasRedis}}
REDIS_URL=redis://redis:6379/0
{{- end}}
{{- if .IsGrpc}}
DOMAIN=api.example.com
{{- else}}
TUNNEL_TOKEN=CHANGE_ME
{{- end}}
```
{{- if .IsGrpc}}

## 3. Domain

Cloudflare tunnels can't serve gRPC on a public hostname, so Caddy serves it from the box on ports 80 and 443.

Point an A record for `DOMAIN` at the box's public IP (`terraform output public_ip`). If the domain is on Cloudflare, set the record to DNS only (grey cloud). On first start Caddy gets a Let's Encrypt certificate, which needs ports 80 and 443 reachable, and keeps it in the `caddydata` volume. Clients connect to `DOMAIN:443` over TLS.
{{- else}}

## 3. Cloudflare tunnel

The domain's DNS must be on Cloudflare. In Zero Trust → Networks → Tunnels, create a tunnel and put its token in `TUNNEL_TOKEN`. Add a public hostname (e.g. `api.example.com`) with service `http://{{.Name}}:4000`. Cloudflare creates a proxied CNAME to the tunnel and serves HTTPS for it. `cloudflared` connects out from the box, so no inbound ports are open.
{{- end}}

## 4. Deploy

Actions → Deploy {{.Environment}} → Run workflow.
{{- else}}

## 1. Once per AWS account

Every environment's deploy role trusts this provider, and AWS allows one per account:

```sh
aws iam create-open-id-connect-provider \
  --url https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com \
  --client-id-list sts.amazonaws.com
```

Then request an ACM certificate for your domain in the same region and validate it. The load balancer only serves HTTPS.

## 2. Apply

Fill in `deployments/{{.Environment}}/terraform/terraform.tfvars`. It's gitignored, like `.env`.

```sh
cd deployments/{{.Environment}}/terraform
terraform init
terraform apply
```

The services start on a placeholder image until the first deploy.
{{- if .IsGrpc}} The placeholder only speaks HTTP/1, so its tasks fail the gRPC health check and ECS keeps restarting them until the first deploy; that is expected.{{end}}
{{- if .HasPostgres}} The database password is generated and stored in Secrets Manager; it is also in the terraform state, so keep the state private.{{end}}

Point your domain at `terraform output load_balancer_dns_name`.

## 3. GitHub environment

```sh
gh api -X PUT repos/OWNER/REPO/environments/{{.Environment}}
for v in aws_role_arn aws_region aws_private_subnets aws_app_security_group; do
  gh variable set "$(echo "$v" | tr a-z A-Z)" --env {{.Environment}} --body "$(terraform output -raw "$v")"
done
```

These are variables, not secrets: the workflow logs in with GitHub OIDC, and the cloud only trusts runs from the `{{.Environment}}` environment of this repo.

## 4. App secrets

Anything the app reads that terraform doesn't know about (e.g. the JWT secret from `ngo auth`) goes into the task definition `secrets`, backed by Secrets Manager.

## 5. Deploy

Actions → Deploy {{.Environment}} → Run workflow. It builds and pushes the images, {{if .HasPostgres}}runs the migration job, {{end}}then rolls out the app.
{{- end}}
{{- if .HasPostgres}}

## Migrations

`build/migrate.sh` runs `goose up`. If it fails, it rolls back to the version it started from and exits non-zero, and the new app is not deployed. That means:

- every migration needs a working `-- +goose Down`
- migrations must be additive, so the running app keeps working on the new schema
- `-- +goose NO TRANSACTION` files (e.g. `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY`) can fail halfway and leave state behind; a failed concurrent index stays as an invalid index you have to drop
{{- end}}

## Notes

- Terraform state is local. Move it to the commented-out backend in `main.tf` before anyone else applies.
